===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sed-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|setten|}}, from {{inh|en|ang|settan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sattjan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*satjaną}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*sodéyeti}}, causative of {{m|ine-pro|*sed-||to sit}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|sets|setting|set|past_ptc2=setten|past_ptc2_qual=dialectal}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[put]] (something) down, to [[rest]].
#: {{syn|en|put|lay|set down}}
#: {{ant|en|pick up}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Set''' the [[tray]] there.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To attach or affix (something) to something else, or in or upon a certain place.
#: {{ux|en|I have '''set''' my heart on running the marathon.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Genesis|4|15|passage=The Lord '''set''' a mark upon Cain.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put in a specified condition or state; to cause to be.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Deuteronomy|28|1|passage=The Lord thy God will '''set''' thee on high.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|10|35|passage=I am come to '''set''' a man at variance against his father, and the daughter against her mother.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1827|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|title=Hamlet
|passage=Every incident '''sets''' him thinking.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[start]] (a [[fire]]).
#: {{syn|en|light}}
#: {{ant|en|extinguish|put out|quench}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|dated}} To cause to stop or stick; to obstruct; to fasten to a spot.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' a coach in the mud}}
# {{senseid|en|determine}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To [[determine]] or [[settle]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' the rent}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[adjust]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''set''' the alarm at 6 a.m.}}  (i.e. I programmed it at that hour to go off at a later time)
#: {{ux|en|I '''set''' the alarm for 6 a.m.}}  (i.e. I programmed it earlier to go off at that hour.)
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To punch (a [[nail]]) into [[wood]] so that its head is below the surface.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To arrange with [[dish]]es and [[cutlery]], to [[set the table]].
#: {{ux|en|Please '''set''' the table for our guests.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[introduce]] or [[describe]].
#: {{ux|en|I’ll tell you what happened, but first let me '''set''' the scene.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|book=III|chapter=II|passage=An incident which happened about this time will '''set''' the characters of these two lads more fairly before the discerning reader than is in the power of the longest dissertation.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} {{infl of|en|set||ed-form}} To [[locate]] (a play, etc.); to assign a [[backdrop]] to, geographically or temporally.
#: {{ux|en|He says he will '''set''' his next film in France.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her debut novel is '''set''' during the U.S. Civil War.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} {{infl of|en|set||ed-form}} To [[compile]], to make (a puzzle or challenge).
#: {{ux|en|This crossword was '''set''' by Araucaria.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To prepare (a stage or film set).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fit (someone) up in a situation. <!-- Same as "set the scene" or rather "prepare"? An example would help.-->
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To arrange (type).
#: {{ux|en|It was a complex page, but he '''set''' it quickly.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[devise]] and [[assign]] (work) to.
#: {{ux|en|The teacher '''set''' her students the task of drawing a foot.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|volleyball}} To direct (the ball) to a teammate for an attack.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[solidify]].
#: {{ux|en|The glue '''sets''' in five minutes.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To render stiff or solid; especially, to convert into curd; to curdle.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' milk for cheese}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a heavenly body, to disappear below the [[horizon]] of a planet, etc, as the latter rotates.<!--not necessarily Earth: on the moon, the Earth can be seen to set-->
#: {{ux|en|The moon '''sets''' at eight o'clock tonight.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|bridge}} To [[defeat]] a [[contract]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|now followed by "out", as in [[set out]]}} To begin to move; to [[go forth]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|text=The king is '''set''' from London, and the scene is now transported, gentles, to Southampton}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|botany}} To produce after pollination.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' seed}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Daniel Chamovitz|title=What a Plant Knows|page=155
|passage=Many fruit trees will only flower and '''set''' fruit following a cold winter.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of fruit}} To be fixed for growth; to strike root; to begin to germinate or form.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1906|author=Canada. Dept. of Agriculture. Fruit Branch|title=Fruit crop report
|passage=In the Annapolis Valley, in spite of an irregular bloom, the fruit has '''set''' well and has, as yet, been little affected by scab.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|Southern US|Midwestern US|dialects}} To [[sit]] {{gloss|be in a seated position}}.
#: {{ux|en|He '''sets''' in that chair all day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=w:Joseph C. Lincoln|chapter=7
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]
|passage=Old Applegate, in the stern, just '''set''' and looked at me, and Lord James, amidship, waved both arms and kept hollering for help. I took a couple of everlasting big strokes and managed to grab hold of the skiff's rail, close to the stern.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987|author=Toni Morrison|title=Beloved|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sfmp6gjZGP8C&pg=PA227|page=227
|passage=And if Mrs. Garner didn't need me right there in the kitchen, I could get a chair and you and me could '''set''' out there while I did the vegetables.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|Southern US|Midwestern US|dialects}} To [[rest]] or [[lie]] somewhere, on something, etc.; to occupy a certain place.
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1975|lyricist=C.W. McCall; Chip Davis|title={{w|Convoy (song)|Convoy}}|album=Black Bear Road|url=|artist=w:C. W. McCall|passage=Well, we rolled up Interstate 44<br>Like a rocket-sled on rails.<br>We tore up all of our swindle sheets<br>And left 'em '''settin'''' on the scales.}}
# To hunt game with the aid of a [[setter]].
# {{lb|en|hunting|ambitransitive}} Of a dog, to indicate the position of [[game]].
#: {{ux|en|The dog '''sets''' the bird.}}
#: {{ux|en|Your dog '''sets''' well.}}
# To apply oneself; to undertake earnestly.
#* {{RQ:Hammond Of Fundamentals
|passage=If he '''set''' industriously and sincerely to perform the commands of Christ, he can have no ground of doubting but it shall prove successful to him.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To fit music to words.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Mac Flecknoe|passage='''Set''' thy own songs, and sing them to thy lute.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|1|2|passage=That I might sing it (Madam) to a tune:<br>Giue me a Note, your Ladiship can '''set'''}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To place plants or shoots in the ground; to plant.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' pear trees in an orchard}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1774|author=John Robinson; Thomas Rispin|title=A Journey Through Nova-Scotia Containing a Particular Account of the Country and its Inhabitants|location=York|publisher=C. Etherington|pageurl=https://hdl.handle.net/2027/aeu.ark:/13960/t56d6f521?urlappend=%3Bseq=24|page=19
|passage=Mr. Forster, from Newcastle, made a purchase here last year. We saw him with eight men '''setting''' potatoes within a week of mid-summer.}}
# To become [[fixed]] or [[rigid]]; to be [[fastened]].
# To have a certain direction of motion; to flow; to move on; to tend.
#: {{ux|en|The current '''sets''' to the north; the tide '''sets''' to the windward.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|country dancing}} To acknowledge a dancing partner by facing him or her and moving first to one side and then to the other, while she or he does the opposite.
#: {{ux|en|'''Set''' to partners! was the next instruction from the caller.}}
# To place or fix in a [[setting]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' a precious stone in a border of metal}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' glass in a sash}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar|58|And him too rich a jewel to be '''set''' / In vulgar metal for a vulgar use.}}
# To put in order in a particular manner; to prepare.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' (that is, to hone) a razor}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' a saw}}
# To extend and bring into position; to spread.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' the sails of a ship}}
# To give a pitch to, as a tune; to start by fixing the keynote.
#* {{RQ:Fielding Joseph Andrews
|passage=I should be very willing to be his clerk; for which you know I am qualified, being able to read, and to '''set''' a psalm}}
# To reduce from a dislocated or fractured state.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' a broken bone}}
# {{lb|en|masonry}} To lower into place and fix solidly, as the blocks of cut stone in a structure.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To wager in gambling; to risk.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|V|iv|passage=I have '''set''' my life upon a cast, / And I will stand the hazard of the die.}}
# To adorn with something infixed or affixed; to stud; to variegate with objects placed here and there.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|The Flower and the Leaf
|passage=High on their heads, with jewels richly '''set''', / Each lady wore a radiant coronet.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1815|author=w:William Wordsworth|title=Poems of the Imagination
|passage=pastoral dales thin '''set''' with modern farms}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To value; to rate; used with ''at''.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|V|ii|passage=Be you contented, wearing now the garland, / To have a son '''set''' your decrees at naught.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|I|iv|passage=I do not '''set''' my life at a pin's fee.}}
# To establish as a rule; to furnish; to prescribe; to assign.
#: {{ux|en|to '''set''' a good example}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} To suit; to become.
#: {{ux|en|It '''sets''' him ill.}}

