===Etymology 4===
From {{der|en|enm|set}}, {{m|enm|sete}}, {{m|enm|sette||that which is set, the act of setting, seat}}, from {{der|en|ang|set||setting, seat, a place where people remain, habitation, camp, entrenchment, a place where animals are kept, stall, fold}} and {{der|en|ang|seten||a set, shoot, slip, branch; a nursery, plantation; that which is planted or set; a cultivated place; planting, cultivation; a setting, putting; a stopping; occupied land}}, related to {{cog|ang|settan||to set}}. Compare {{cog|gml|gesette||a set, suite}}, {{cog|ang|gesetl||assembly}}. According to Skeat, in senses denoting a group of things or persons, representing an alteration of {{m|en|sept}}, from {{der|en|fro|sette||a religious sect}}, from {{der|en|ML.|secta||retinue}}, from {{der|en|la|secta||a faction}}. See {{l|en|sect}}. It is quite possible that the modern word is more of a merger between both, however.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A young plant fit for setting out; a [[slip]]; [[shoot]].
# A rudimentary fruit.
# The setting of the sun or other luminary; {{lb|en|by extension}} the close of the day.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=I|poem=Adeline|page=41|passage=And a rose-bush leans upon, / Thou that faintly smilest still, / As a Naiad in a well, / Looking at the '''set''' of day, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|act=V|scene=iii|passage=The weary sun hath made a golden '''set'''.}}
# {{lb|en|literally|and|figuratively}} General [[movement]]; [[direction]]; [[drift]]; [[tendency]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1840|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|title=Style
|passage=Here and there, amongst individuals alive to the particular evils of the age, and watching the very '''set''' of the current, there may have been even a more systematic counteraction applied to the mischief.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1951|author=Herman Wouk|title=The Caine Mutiny|page=238|passage=He put his eye to the alidade. “I thought so! Zero ''five'' four and that's allowing nothing for '''set''' and drift along the line of bearing. We're inside the departure point now{{...}}”}}
# A [[match]]ing [[collection]] of similar things. (''Note the similar meaning in [[#Noun|Etymology 2, Noun]]''.)
#: {{ux|en|a '''set''' of tables}}
# A collection of various objects for a particular purpose.
#: {{ux|en|a '''set''' of tools}}
# An object made up of several parts.
#: {{ux|en|a '''set''' of steps}}
# {{senseid|en|math-collection}}{{lb|en|set theory}} A collection of zero or more [[object]]s, possibly infinite in size, and disregarding any order or repetition of the objects which may be contained within it.
# {{lb|en|in plural, “sets”|mathematics|informal}} [[set theory|Set theory]].
# A group of people, usually meeting socially.
#: {{ux|en|the country '''set'''}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Pendennis|chapter=XIX|passage=That he took perfumed baths is a truth; and he used to say that he took them after meeting certain men of a very low '''set''' in hall.}}
# The [[scenery]] for a [[film]] or [[play]].
# the general [[locations]] and [[area]] where a [[movie]]’s, a [[film]]’s, or  a [[video]]’s [[scenery]] is arranged to be filmed also including places for [[actors]], assorted crew, [[director]], [[producer]]s which are typically not filmed.
# {{lb|en|dance}} The initial or basic [[formation]] of [[dancer]]s.
# {{senseid|en|exercise}} {{lb|en|exercise}} A group of [[repetition]]s of a single [[exercise]] performed one after the other without rest.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1974|author=Charles Gaines; George Butler|title=Pumping Iron: The Art and Sport of Bodybuilding|page=22
|passage=This is the fourth '''set''' of benchpresses.}}
# {{senseid|en|tennis}} {{lb|en|tennis}} A complete series of [[game]]s, forming part of a [[match]].
# {{lb|en|volleyball}} A complete series of [[point]]s, forming part of a [[match]].
